[Black "Black study side"]
[Result "*"]

{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 e6 4. O-O Be7 5. d3 O-O 6. Nbd2 c5 7. e4 Nc6 8. Re1 Qc7 9. c3 h6 10. Qe2 Rd8 { Laboratory starts here. Re1 supports e4 and makes e5 more credible. Black has a broad d5-c5 center and may expand on the queenside while White builds a kingside initiative. Check whether e5 is safe, then consider Nf1 and h4 only if the center is stable. White's queen bishop needs a route, often through f4 or g5 after development. } 11. a3 { Candidate comparison. Controls nearby wing squares and may restrain a piece or pawn. Compare this useful purpose with the time lost to central development. } 11... Rb8 12. e5 Nd7 13. Nf1 b5 14. Bf4 Nf8 15. h4 { Candidate comparison. Creates a wing or central pawn contact and changes king shelter. Calculate captures and the opponent's central counterplay before extending the advance. } 15... Ng6 16. Bd2 a5 17. N1h2 b4 18. axb4 axb4 19. d4 { Candidate comparison. Changes central space and pawn contacts. Calculate captures, advances and maintaining tension before choosing the next piece plan. } 19... Bd7 20. Bf1 Rdc8 *

