{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 e6 4. O-O Be7 5. d3 O-O 6. Nbd2 c5 7. e4 dxe4 8. dxe4 b5 9. e5 Nd5 10. Re1 Bb7 { Laboratory starts here. The central exchange removes the familiar d5-e5 blockade. White's d-pawn becomes an e-pawn, opening the d-file and changing the bishop's prospects. Develop on open files and compare piece activity. Use the e4 outpost control and consider Nc4 when it creates a real threat. } 11. Qe2 { Candidate comparison. Places the queen on e2. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 11... c4 12. Ne4 Nd7 13. a4 a6 14. Bg5 Rb8 15. axb5 { Candidate comparison. The pawn captures a pawn on b5. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 15... axb5 16. Rad1 Qc7 17. Nd4 Nxe5 18. Bxe7 Nxe7 19. Nxb5 { Candidate comparison. The knight captures a pawn on b5. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 19... Qb6 20. Ned6 Bxg2 *

