{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 c6 4. O-O Bf5 5. d3 e6 6. Nh4 Bg6 7. Nxg6 hxg6 8. Nd2 Bd6 9. e4 Nbd7 10. d4 Nxe4 { Laboratory starts here. White gives a knight for the active bishop and changes Black's kingside pawn structure. The open h-file can help Black as well as create targets. Develop the remaining pieces and use the bishop pair in a position that may open. Do not assume doubled pawns alone justify a direct attack. } 11. Nxe4 { Candidate comparison. The knight captures a knight on e4. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 11... dxe4 12. Bxe4 O-O 13. c3 Qc7 14. a4 e5 15. Bg2 { Candidate comparison. Completes the kingside fianchetto. Trace the diagonal to its first blocker; the bishop cannot attack through friendly pawns or pieces. } 15... a5 16. dxe5 Nxe5 17. Qb3 Nd3 18. Bg5 Be7 19. Be3 { Candidate comparison. Places the bishop on e3. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 19... Bc5 20. Be4 Bxe3 *

