{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 c5 3. Bg2 Nc6 4. O-O e5 5. d3 Nf6 6. e4 d4 7. a4 Bd6 8. Nfd2 a6 9. f4 h6 10. Qe2 Be6 { Laboratory starts here. Black closes the center with a space advantage. White has a reversed King's Indian type of position, but the move order and extra tempo require independent calculation. Prepare a kingside lever with appropriate knight moves or use c3 to question d4. Choose a side based on the fixed pawn chain and king locations. } 11. Kh1 { Candidate comparison. Places the king on h1. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 11... b5 12. Nf3 c4 13. fxe5 Bxe5 14. Bf4 Nd7 15. Nbd2 { Candidate comparison. Develops the queen knight while leaving the c-pawn free. The knight supports e4 and may seek c4 or a route through f1. } 15... O-O 16. axb5 axb5 17. dxc4 Rxa1 18. Rxa1 bxc4 19. Nxc4 { Candidate comparison. The knight captures a pawn on c4. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 19... Bxf4 20. gxf4 Qf6 *

