{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 c5 2. g3 Nc6 3. Bg2 g6 4. O-O Bg7 5. d3 d6 6. e4 Nf6 7. a4 O-O 8. Nc3 Rb8 9. h3 b6 10. Nh2 a6 { Laboratory starts here. Starting with Nf3 allows Black a Sicilian-like or English-like structure. White can establish e4 after seeing Black's fianchetto. Finish development with Nbd2 or Nc3 according to the desired pawn break. Decide whether c3-d4 or c4 gives the pieces the best squares. } 11. Nd5 { Candidate comparison. Places the knight on d5, attacking knight on f6. A legal capture may still be unsound if the target is defended. } 11... b5 12. axb5 axb5 13. Bg5 b4 14. Nxf6+ exf6 15. Bf4 { Candidate comparison. Develops the queen bishop with central influence. Check whether a pawn advance can gain a tempo against it. } 15... Be6 16. c3 Rc8 17. Ng4 d5 18. Qf3 bxc3 19. bxc3 { Candidate comparison. The pawn captures a pawn on c3. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 19... d4 20. c4 Ne5 *

