{ Original King's Indian Attack course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 c5 3. Bg2 Nc6 4. O-O e5 5. d3 Nf6 6. c4 d4 7. e3 Be7 8. exd4 cxd4 9. Bg5 a5 10. Re1 h6 { Laboratory starts here. White chooses c4 before e4 to challenge the broad center. Once Black closes with ...d4, the result belongs to a different structure from the main KIA attack. Decide between e3 to undermine d4 and queenside development. Keep the g2 bishop's diagonal in mind when opening the center. } 11. Bxf6 { Candidate comparison. The bishop captures a knight on f6. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 11... Bxf6 12. c5 Be6 13. Na3 a4 14. Rc1 Bxa2 15. Nc4 { Candidate comparison. Places the knight on c4. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 15... O-O 16. Nfxe5 Bxe5 17. Nxe5 Bd5 18. Nxc6 bxc6 19. Qh5 { Candidate comparison. Places the queen on h5, attacking bishop on d5. A legal capture may still be unsound if the target is defended. } 19... a3 20. bxa3 Rxa3 *

